      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 Wolters Kluwer Health | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "High levels of soluble receptor for advanced glycation end products (sRAGE) have been shown to have an atheroprotective role; however, no data are available on this molecule in acute coronary syndromes (ACS). We evaluated sRAGE levels in patients with non-ST segment elevation ACS (NSTE-ACS) or with chronic stable angina."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We studied 265 patients, 190 of whom had NSTE-ACS and 75 had chronic stable angina."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Plasma sRAGE values were comparable in the two groups (P=0.19). However, in the patients with NSTE-ACS, sRAGE levels were significantly higher in patients with cardiac troponin-I (cTnI) of more than or equal to 0.04 µg/l compared with those with cTnI of less than 0.04 µg/l [758 (493-1536 ) pg/ml vs. 454 (167-899) pg/ml; P=0.0037]. A significant correlation (r=0.323, P=0.0045) was found between sRAGE and cTnI levels in patients with NSTE-ACS."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Plasma sRAGE levels are elevated in patients with NSTE-ACS with positive cTnI, suggesting that they could be related to myocardial cell damage."
          }
        ]
      },
